WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:08.139
Dashboards for Indigo. Here is a look round a real house. Everything you see is live, and every press is real.

2
00:00:09.839 --> 00:00:17.263
This is the hub, the page that lives on the wall tablet. It answers one question first: is the house all right?

3
00:00:17.713 --> 00:00:24.027
The pills say who is home, how many radiators are calling for heat, and whether anything needs a look.

4
00:00:24.477 --> 00:00:28.019
Favourites are one-tap tiles. Press the garage door,

5
00:00:28.469 --> 00:00:32.714
and the tile turns blue and counts the seconds while the door moves.

6
00:00:34.919 --> 00:00:40.529
It turns red once the door is fully open, and the live camera strip shows it happening.

7
00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:50.591
Every room has its own page. The garage has its cameras, and the door, which is open now.

8
00:00:51.041 --> 00:00:55.137
Press to close, and the tile follows the door all the way down.

9
00:00:55.587 --> 00:00:59.470
It goes quiet again once the sensors say the door is shut.

10
00:01:14.137 --> 00:01:16.271
Tap any camera on the hub,

11
00:01:17.371 --> 00:01:21.317
and the Cameras page opens with every camera playing live.

12
00:01:21.767 --> 00:01:27.463
The big picture follows whichever one you tap. Tap the front door, and it swaps to the top.

13
00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:38.379
Further down the hub are the power flow, today's solar and the weather station. More on the energy in a moment.

14
00:01:38.829 --> 00:01:45.677
Below them, today's totals, what the solar has saved in pounds, and the weather from the station on the roof.

15
00:01:46.127 --> 00:01:53.380
At the bottom, a list of any open doors and windows, then tiles for the menu and the rooms. The menu first.

16
00:01:54.680 --> 00:02:01.912
The menu groups every page by the question it answers: what is on right now, and then the energy and the cost.

17
00:02:02.362 --> 00:02:10.127
Then what happened, with a timeline that replays any day, and whether anything is wrong, from alerts to the server itself.

18
00:02:10.577 --> 00:02:19.559
Setup holds the settings, and any web page can be added as a tile, such as the UniFi controller, so one menu leads to everything.

19
00:02:20.009 --> 00:02:23.081
Rooms has a page for every room in the house.

20
00:02:24.381 --> 00:02:30.610
Every room gets its own page, with lights, sensors, radiators and doors. Pick the kitchen.

21
00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.291
In the kitchen, switch the spot lights on,

22
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.784
and slide them down to half brightness.

23
00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.469
All On switches on every light in the room.

24
00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:48.649
A room can leave a light out of that group, such as a main light that keeps its own switch.

25
00:02:49.099 --> 00:02:51.510
And All Off puts them all out again.

26
00:02:52.549 --> 00:02:58.480
Below the lights are the room's motion sensors, its temperature and humidity, and the window.

27
00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.233
The living room works the same way.

28
00:03:02.683 --> 00:03:05.371
In the living room, switch the colour lamp on,

29
00:03:06.133 --> 00:03:08.587
and slide it down to half brightness.

30
00:03:09.583 --> 00:03:14.021
All On switches on every light in the group, and that includes the fire.

31
00:03:14.471 --> 00:03:19.484
The main light is left out of the group on purpose, so it keeps its own switch.

32
00:03:19.934 --> 00:03:22.345
And All Off puts them all out again.

33
00:03:24.634 --> 00:03:35.045
The Heating page draws its data from the Evohome heating plugin: every radiator with its temperature and setpoint, and a boost for the whole house at the top.

34
00:03:35.495 --> 00:03:44.263
We are still in summer mode here, so every radiator is held at eight degrees. The boost comes into its own once the heating is needed again.

35
00:03:44.713 --> 00:03:52.009
Scrolling down shows the rest of the radiators, with the coldest and warmest rooms picked out in blue and amber.

36
00:03:53.709 --> 00:04:01.602
Right now the sun is making far more than the house needs, so the spare is charging the battery, which is 66 percent full.

37
00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:09.151
The dots show which way the power is going, and they speed up as more of it flows.

38
00:04:09.601 --> 00:04:25.089
Underneath are how self-sufficient the house has been today, 0 percent so far, with a note when an overnight cheap-rate charge is why it reads low, the solar against today's forecast, what it has saved in pounds, and the battery's charge.

39
00:04:25.539 --> 00:04:34.520
Each hour's solar is split by roof, and set against a dashed mark for what the forecast promised. The pale bars are the hours still to come.

40
00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:43.546
The battery section shows what it is doing now, its last day as a line, and its health, down to the temperature and balance of the cells.

41
00:04:43.996 --> 00:04:48.156
And When to run it plans the washing around the sun and the battery.

42
00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:54.421
Scenes puts every Indigo action group on a button, grouped by folder.

43
00:04:56.121 --> 00:05:03.140
Alerts gathers the errors in Indigo's log, and lets you add your own rules, sent by Pushover or email.

44
00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:13.075
WiFi shows every access point and every client, and flags a crowded channel or an old device slowing the rest down.

45
00:05:14.775 --> 00:05:21.409
And Mains lists every meter in the house, with how far they disagree with each other and with the inverter.

46
00:05:22.709 --> 00:05:25.589
Every page you have seen began as a sentence.

47
00:05:26.039 --> 00:05:34.722
Say what you want on a page, in plain English, and Claude builds it. Lights, doors, heating, cameras, anything Indigo can switch or show.

48
00:05:35.172 --> 00:05:40.783
Your house is different, so ask for your own pages. The only limit is your imagination.
